Mazaa is a Hindi-Urdu word that refers to the embodied experiences of fun, pleasure and play. 
Mazaa resides in small acts; it lurks undercover, just below the surface of the humdrum, 
quotidian tenor of responsible life – A stolen glance across the train compartment, a brief brush 
of fingers, a forbidden touch or an illicit meeting under cover of darkness. Mazaa is what makes 
life tolerable; mazaa is what makes life fun.  

This text-based game invites players into the social worlds of hijras, kohtis, dhurranis1, 
and their illicit affairs. For working-class people in South Asia, public transport structures 
everyday life: countless hours are spent moving between work and home in overcrowded buses, 
trains, and metros. Every morning, hundreds of bodies spill into cramped compartments, pressed 
together by heat, fatigue, and proximity. In that dense choreography of movement, glances are 
exchanged and hands begin to wander. Mazaa invites you to feel the anticipation, the adrenaline, 
the anxiety, and the fleeting pleasures of cruising in the streets of Kolkata. 
